Bill Zettler wrote:

> Install the 'mkisofs' package (included in Red Hat 6.0), which will enable
> you to create a complete iso image out of the file system you wish to
> burn.

In case you don't have a running Linux system than you can also get Win32
mkisofs binaries. Just do an ftpsearch for mkisofs*.exe.

>  mkisofs -a -R -l -o myimage.iso .   (including the . at the end - it's not
> a period)
>
> (-a = all files, -R = Rock Ridge ext, -l = long filenames, -o = output
> file name)

If you add the T (transbl) option it will be easier for other OSs to read the
CD with the RR extensions. You can also use the v (verbose) mode which makes
it easier in case of any errors but I dunno if it works with the W32 binaries.
It would be

mkisofs -lRTv -o myimage.iso /path

Dirk




