Eberhard Burr <Eberhard.Burr@gmx.de> wrote in message
news:87k8o81thf.fsf@gmx.de...
> "David Hocking" <davidh@student.unsw.edu.au> writes:
>
> > Hi,
> > I'm fairly new to linux and have finally got Debian loaded ok. Although
when
> > good choice :-) i try to run startx it tells me that /dev/mouse isn't a
file or
> > directory.
> >
> > So can anyone tell me how you load a mouse driver or something so that
> > /dev/mouse is "mounted" (or loaded).
>
> On Debian, afaik, /dev/mouse isn't created automatically unless you
> install gpm, the general purpose mouse support daemon. If you only
> want to use the mouse under X, then the best thing to do is to load
> the file /etx/X11/XF86Config into an editor and replace the reference
> to /dev/mouse by /dev/ttyS0 (if your mouse lives on COM1 under DOS) or
> /dev/ttyS1 (COM2 under DOS) or /dev/psaux (mouse on the PS/2 mouse
> port)

Actually, /dev/mouse is a symbolic link to whatever device file you are
using as a mouse. It is not created automatically :-( but that can be
easily corrected. Just create a symbolic link called /dev/mouse
referring to /dev/ttyS0 (mouse on COM1) or /dev/ttyS1 (mouse on COM2)
or /dev/psaux (mouse on PS/2 mouse port), etc. That way, you can refer
to the mouse as /dev/mouse, without constantly having to know where
the mouse is.

Martin Sinot
martin@spase.nl





