I have an AlphaPC 164LX motherboard I recently acquired which has an
L3 cache problem. The board has the 2MB of SRAM cache installed, but
if enabled the system will not boot (boots fine with cache disabled
and runs OK, but of course serverely crippled...). I have experienced
similar problems on x86 systems and replaced socketed SRAM DIPs and
all was well. The 164LX motherboard SRAM chips are soldered in and
this inrcreases the difficulty of the repair a great deal. I suspect
replacing only the one chip that has a bad memory element will yield a
system that boots with a full complement of cache 8^)... That said, I
am looking for a diagnostic program that can be executed from the
Alpha BIOS setup that could perform a test of the cache subsystem that
would return an address intercept that I could use to reveal which of
the chips (If not all of them!) is bad. I might not be aware that I
could get an extended POST output from the board which does exectly
what I want. As it is now, the only thing the board does when I
attempt to boot with the cache enabled is beep the speaker six times
in a row continuously which according to the documentation means a
"checksum error [was] detected when image was read back from memory."
I would like more information than this -- alas, it may not be
possible... Any thoughts or comments would be appreciated. I have
managed to locate a possible source for the SRAM chips, so I'll be
checking to see what hoops I have to jump through to get a small
quantity of them this week.  All else aside, the final challenge will
be removing and installing the chips. Seems like a shame to have to
run the system in this condition since the cache would really
supercharge the system's performance. I really would like to pull this
off somehow... 

