Guillem Campos <gcampos@coyote.asoc.euitt.upm.es> writes:

>I have installed RH 5.2 on an AS/400 (I'm expecting the
>oportunity to install RH 6.x or debian, or something) and
>kernel compilation is making me sick. I have tried 2.0.36
>(the one that comes with RH 5.2) 2.2.3, 2.2.12 and today
>2.2.13, and with all of them I get the same: when compiling
>files in arch/alpha/math-emu/ gcc upsets and says 'internal
>compiler error: cc1 got signal 6' after a lot of warnings
>about 'left shift count is negative' and so on. I'm new
>to alpha but I have compiled many kernels on x86 without
>any problem.
>What is happening here? Gcc is broke? Gods are against me?
>Please help!

(... long silence ...)  Ahem.  You're not alone.  There's two of us.

It must be a compiler bug (internal compiler error, signal 6).

The negative shift counts are a red herring.   They're just warnings.
They come from a macro that expands like this:

    if(1<64) {
	yadda, yadda << (64-1) yadda, yadda
    } else {
	yadda, yadda << (1-64) yadda, yadda
    }

The `1' was a parameter to the macro.  The `else' branch is not taken,
but the constant (1-64) is still folded at compile-time and produces
the warning.  The compiler trips somewhere else,  I have no idea where.
This is on redhat 5.2, egcs-1.0.3a-14 .

You say you have the same problem with older kernels?  For me this
started at 2.2.12 .  My workaround was to fall back to 2.2.11 and apply
the errata (patches) from http://www.linux.org.uk .  Also a TCP sequence
number patch from
http://kernelnotes.org/lnxlists/linux-kernel/lk_9909_04/msg00664.html .

Is your source tree clean?  Try unpacking a fresh distribution of 2.2.11
somewhere.  You don't need to untar in /usr/src to compile the kernel.
(Some include files have to be in /usr/src/linux/include to *install*
the kernel, but that's another issue.)

The clean fix would be to upgrade the compiler.  Unfortunately, the
release notes of 2.2.13 say *not* to compile stable kernels with gcc 2.95 .

--
--Pierre Asselin, Westminster, Colorado
  lpa@netcom.com



