Uriah Queen wrote:
> 
> On Tue, 19 Oct 1999, Jeff Sullivan wrote:
> 
> > > Does anyone have a list of 'Stable' compilers for Alpha Linux?
> >
> > One option would be the Compaq C and Fortran compilers that are
> > now in Beta test. Compaq C++ should be available in the future.
> > See http://www.unix.digital.com/linux/software.htm for more
> > information.
> 
> I thought of that, but unfortunutaly I'm more inclined to want the C/C++
> pair rather than the C/Fortran pair.  Also, seeing how the compiler is
> listed as beta, how well does it generate byte code?  Dont take me wronge,
> I do belive that Compaq would not release such a project and have it
> spitting out teeth all over the place, but Ive always shyed away from
> beta's...

As far as ccc's beta status, it is useful to note that it is a port
from the Tru64 UNIX C compiler, from the DigitalUNIX, formerly OSF/1...
In other words, the compiler is a well developed piece of software,
its beta status is from the recent port to Linux, not instability or
generating bad opcodes.  You should expect a few problems with compiling
large packages (like the kernel), but for smaller, not as wierd ones
it works fine.  Better than fine, it kicks gcc's ass at optimization.

I hear that they are working on C++, I don't know when it will be done.
Maybe they'll generate precompiled headers?  I hear people complaining
about that one a lot with gcc.

Remember, Copmaq is not doing this out of altruism; they want to sell
you Alpha computers.  Lots of Alpha computers.  Thanks to these compilers,
I'm able to do about 2 times as much number crunching as with a similarly
priced Intel box.

My 2 cents...

