Raymonds Doetjes (rdoetjes@node102bd.a2000.nl) wrote:
: May I ask what the best buffersize is?

The bigest one you can have without degrading the performance of
your system, but not bigger that 700Mb or whereabouts ;-)

Or seriously: it depends on how are you going to use cdrecord.
With a 4x recorder, a four megabyte buffer gives you a cushion 
to survive a three-second hiccup in the data feed going into 
cdrecord. This is plenty for recording image files from a local
hard drive, so if you'll only do that, 4Mb is all you need.

If you are planning to record CDs with slower or less predictable
sources (like a pipe from mkisofs, or an image file on an NFS
file system, or, the worst combination, a pipe from mkisofs running
over an NFS file system ;-)), you'll probably have to increase the 
buffer size.

In fact, cdrecord gives you an indication on how well your buffer
is tuned to your situation: at the end of the recording session,
it will print the lowest low watermark in the buffer during the
recording session. If this value gets low (with "low" being 
determined by the variance in the feed speed), you'll have to
increase buffer size to ensure reliable recording sessions.
I'd say that having the low water mark below 50% is already too
low - but then I tend to be overly cautious ;-)

Cheers,

/Serge.P

