"David Hocking" <davidh@student.unsw.edu.au> writes:

> Hi,
> I'm fairly new to linux and have finally got Debian loaded ok. Although when

good choice :-)

> i try to run startx it tells me that /dev/mouse isn't a file or directory.
> 
> So can anyone tell me how you load a mouse driver or something so that
> /dev/mouse is "mounted" (or loaded).

On Debian, afaik, /dev/mouse isn't created automatically unless you
install gpm, the general purpose mouse support daemon. If you only
want to use the mouse under X, then the best thing to do is to load
the file /etx/X11/XF86Config into an editor and replace the reference
to /dev/mouse by /dev/ttyS0 (if your mouse lives on COM1 under DOS) or 
/dev/ttyS1 (COM2 under DOS) or /dev/psaux (mouse on the PS/2 mouse
port)
Also, close to the reference to the device, in the "Pointer"-section,
you'll find  a reference to the protocol. Check the setting there too.

If you want any of a) using the mouse under plain console,
b) multiplexing of more than one pointing device onto the X pointer or 
c) using a device not supported by X, then you may want to check out
the gpm package. Beware: the setup-script of gpm is slightly confusing 
but the docs under /usr/doc/gpm are really good.

