X-Trace: typhoon-sf.snfc21.pbi.net 941182032 207.104.3.3 (Fri, 29 Oct 1999 00:27:12 PDT)
Organization: Bill Zettler Consulting
MIME-Version: 1.0
NNTP-Posting-Date: Fri, 29 Oct 1999 00:27:12 PDT
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

Install the 'mkisofs' package (included in Red Hat 6.0), which will enable
you to create a complete iso image out of the file system you wish to
burn. You will need enough disk space to have a second complete copy of
the file system.

A couple minutes' experimentation makes me conclude the correct usage of
'mkisofs' is to cd to the top directory (which will be the root directory
of your iso image) and execute:

mkisofs -a -R -l -o myimage.iso .   (including the . at the end - it's not
a period)

(-a = all files, -R = Rock Ridge ext, -l = long filenames, -o = output
file name)

You can loopback mount the resulting iso image if you want to examine the
contents after mkisofs is done, i.e.,

mount -t iso9660 -o loop myimage.iso /mnt/mountpoint

Now, you can export or remote mount or copy myimage.iso to Windows 98 and
from CDCreator, use File...Create CD from Disc Image...

You have the option of loading .cif or .iso files in this dialog. Choose
the latter, load it in, and cross your fingers!

HTH,
Bill

