I like RedHat.  Granted, I haven't used other AlphaLinux products,
but I've never had any real problems with it.

People seem to come out with either packages designed for RedHat,
or source tarballs.  I know that there are translators, but I've also
heard of problems with them.

I've heard that there are lots more packages that come with SuSE,
so if you need them, go for it.  Nothing is going to be perfect and
SuSE is really popular in Europe, so you can probably find locals
who use it.  Debian and NetBSD are also good systems.  I guess it
comes down to what you need:  SuSE for lots of goodies, RedHat
for simple systems, Debian for OpenSource purists, NetBSD for
security.  (Right, everyone?)

I have to strongly recommend trying out the Compaq compiler/library
suite.  I know that there are other collections of packages out
there (libffm, Goto's BLAS stuff, etc.) which do some of the things
that the Compaq stuff does.  They work pretty well.  Gcc, on the
other hand has not been fully optimized for Alpha.  I've seen anywhere
from 15% to 500% speedups using the Compaq stuff.  They make a
good business case for buying an Alpha in the first place.

Hope this helps.

Tony Hammitt
thammitt@kc.rr.com


P.S.

I'm not trying to start a flamewar.  These are my opinions, you
have your own.



