Probably the simplest thing is to grab the latest src.rpm for teTeX
and 'rpm --rebuild ...'.  If there are no compiler issues it should
take care of itself. :-)  You may want do 'rpm -i ...'; 'rpm -ba ...'
instead as you likely **really** want to see if tests do not show
any problems and 'rpm --rebuild ...' will clean up the whole 
build tree.

: Has anybody done this?

Recompile the whole TeX system? Many folks all over the place.
Or with ccc?  This I do not know.  Recompiling TeX (and passing
'trip' and 'trap' tests afterwards) is a pretty good check on
a compiler and many compiler bugs were caught that way.

What are actually your goals in this exercise?  Remember that
'virtex' and 'virmf' do not really use a system provided floating
point stuff.

