telford@xenon.triode.net.au wrote:

> FIRST question... what is the real reason why it is so slow? I mean

Write-through, direct-mapped L1 data cache.  That's what most other Alphas
have as well, but they have better L2's to compensate for that.

> the chip is 166 MHz but a Pentium 150 is much faster (even when the
> P150 uses slow IDE discs and the Multia uses somewhat faster SCSI).

If you're comparing that by, say, compiling a kernel, then you're in
fact comparing the gcc's code generation for x86 vs. Alpha, rather than
the hardware you're running on.

However, yes, the Multia is quite a bit slower than a P150 for typical
tasks.  There're some where it is faster, though -- for example, those
which need more registers than what x86 offers -- but those are rare.

> The chip is supposed to be able to run two instructions per clock
> while the P150 rarely would achieve two instructions per clock (this is
> a pre-MMX Pentium) so what is the bottleneck?

They both rarely achieve two instructions per cycle (the Pentium might
in fact achieve that more often, due to the availability of assembly
hacks for it in many applications; MP3 playing is an example).

EV4 can only dual-issue one load/store with one arithmetic instruction
(somewhat simplified).  Loads are usually not as common as arithmetic
operations, and stores are very expensive anyway (they get to L2 cache).

> SECOND question... can anyone get sound to record on one of these

Haven't tried, sorry (and I don't have the Multia anymore).

> machines? I have compiled up a 2.2.12 kernel including Microsoft Sound
> System support and I got IRQ and DMA numbers of various Multia web sites.
> Playback works fine, you can play MP3 without a problem (well top goes
> up to about 90% and moving an X11 window around causes the sound to
> skip but the sound is fine if you don't touch the mouse).

MP3 playing (128kbps) was taking something under 40% CPU on mine (with
amp).  The same goes for my HP 9000/712 at 80 MHz now (there's some noise
on the PA-RISC, though, which I didn't bother to investigate, yet; could
be low precision fp ops).  The same is taking 6% CPU (plus 2% for netcat,
and some system time, -- for my remote playing), on 164SX.

> FINAL question, how similar are the various generations of Alpha
> in terms of setup, admin, software development, etc? I mean, if I play

Not very similar, but the concepts are similar enough.

> around on the Multia for a while, am I going to be comfortable going
> to the latest workstation or am I going to have to start learning all
> over again? Also, if I fiddle around with some code so that it runs
> quickly on the Multia, can I assume that it should also be optimal
> for faster Alphas or am I going to find new and interesting bottlenecks

No, you can't assume that.

