Greetings!

	At Slippery Rock University, the Computer Science Department needs to
replace our aging DEC Alpha with a newer model. We have approximately $22,000
to spend, but need some advice as to what is the best direction to go.

	We have approximately 275 computing majors at SRU. Our current Alpha
box supports virtually all Unix use by computer science majors, and Web page
hosting for computer science, information systems, and information technology
majors. (Web-page serving for our majors is expected to grow several-fold.)
Access to the Alpha is via telnet or an NT-hosted X-server; this scheme is only
partially satisfactory. We have created a new Unix lab with 16 Gateway PC's
(Gateway is the official religion of our computer support department) running
Linux, one of which acts as a server for the lab.

	The new (presumably Alpha) server will be on the same LAN segment as
the Unix lab. All other campus labs except one will have to reach these
machines via the campus backbone, currently 1 Mbps FDDI. Bandwidth,
particularly for X, is a concern. The second (first?) god on campus is Windows
NT, and except for our Unix lab and Education's Mac lab, all campus labs use
Windows NT. Enough background; now for the questions:

	1) Does it make sense to use an Alpha server when all other Unix
machines are on Intel processors? What about Sun/SPARC?

	2) If so, what model/configuration/vendor will give the best bang for
the buck. I know $22,000 isn't a lot of money.

	3) Would it make more sense just to let remote users access whatever
Linux PC is under utilized, while using the server for file storage (and
not as an X-client)?

	4) Can you recommend an X-server that runs under Windows NT that is
inexpensive and easy on bandwidth?

	5) What OS should we use? Alpha Linux? DEC's Unix (whatever they're
calling it this week)?

	6) If we use DEC's Unix, what incompatibilities are there between DEC's
C/C++ compilers and the gnu C/C++ compilers of Linux? Are there
incompatibilities between the Gnu Intel compilers and the Gnu Alpha
compilers? I.e., if we are living with two architectures, will our students'
source code be 100% portable between the two platforms?

	7) We want to make our students file trees accessible to them whether
they are logged in as Unix or NT users. What is the best technique for NT/Unix
file sharing? I am leaning toward Samba, but there may be advantages to using
NFS.

	Please pardon the length of this message, but there are many
interdependent questions. Yes, I have searched deja.com, and have found nothing
of any real help. Please feel free to comment on any of the above questions
that you feel confident in answering (or others that you feel that I should have
asked). I am using KDE's krd 1.1.2 for news, and it seems quite flaky (this is
the third time I am composing this message, and I don't think it was
transmitted the previous two times), so please email me a copy of your
response(s). Thank you.

--Mike

