Why use a RAID controller and take a performance blow?
Just use RAID0+RAID1 from kernel level this increases the throughput app 300 to
400% compared to hardware RAID.

You might go and reply that hardware RAID reduces cpu overhead the answer is
absolutly seen yes relatively seen no.
Because when I run a RAID controller with RAID0+RAID1 (this is the fastest and
most secure RAID method) I have a CPU usage of app. 25% (on my intel P166 I
never ran RAID on the Alpha it's a number cruncher no file server :) ).

Under external RAID controller my CPU usage is app. 10% but infact I have 300%
less throughput so when I
want to gain the same throughput I would have about 30% load on my CPU 5% more
in some curcumstances I will even get throughputs of about 400%!

I have abandond RAID5 (wich you guys use) for about 1.5 years since it
generates to much overhead. 2 read and 2 write actions were to expensive for
our fileserver.

So I use 2 RAID0 stripe sets (fast reading for big files) and I mirror those.
In a RAID1 (mirror) I can read from either 2 drives on a different place wich
boosts up reading performance, and the reading performance on the striped set
will make it double.
I get awesome throughputs with this configuration, throughputs you can never
get with RAID5 and using the kernel RAID0 and RAID1 makes it even faster (no
waiting for bus latnecy and CPU responses to handle the read/write action, no
waiting for the raid system to write the data and reply that confirmation).

This is some of my insides on RAID. (I do agree about the SCIS CDROM though)

Raymond

