First of all let me point you to a pro to choose for an Alpha that is the floating
point abilities.
If you do not have much floating point calculations then an Alpha would be out of
place. Since a fileserver well hardly ever use floating-point calculations and
SpecInt on a Alpha is just slightly higher then a Intel machine with the same
clockrate.

The gnu C and C++ compilers are compatible with intel, you only have to bare in mind
that an alpha is a 64bit cpu so writing clean and portable software is slightly
harder because of the 32 and 64 bit type changes. (I never seem to have that problem
though :)

Concidering the OS version, that depends on what you want to do with the server. If
it is mainly a development and fileserver Linux will do (because of the great amount
of development tools for free). If you want to use the server as X-server for
special applications or a Database server then Tru64 (formely dec-unix) is a better
but more expensive choice. You can also run OSF-1 complaint software on Alpha Linux
when you have the shared libs and ofcourse a licence for Tru64. Though this solution
is not really 100% stable ofcourse. I run netscape this way and MSM (Micronetics
Mumps)

About fileserving, it is a logical choice to make the fileserver act as bot a NFS
and SMB server. SMB for the Widow systems and NFS for the Unixlike systems. This way
you can also act as a gateway between the two (though this can generate some delay).

If you are lookiing for a SmallBandwith multi-user NT server to serve Office
products then Windows NT Terminal server with RDP or ICA (wich is better but sold
seperatly) will do the trick.

Raymond

