$22,000 will buy some big iron processing power. The real question is, what power do
you need?  Are any of your CS people doing number-crunching? Adjuncts in the
engineering/science departments need to do PDEs, maybe? Do a lot of 3D graphics? If
not, and you just need some general-purpose Unix machines to program on, I'd have to
recommend stocking a room with cheap x86 machines.

Even if you need the number-crunching, I'd recommend taking the Web server off the
Alpha. Unnecessary processes will just help through your compute-intensive
applications out of cache, and there's no value-added to running Apache on Alpha.

Other than those general thoughts, I have a specific recommendation for your (4)

>         4) Can you recommend an X-server that runs under Windows NT that is
> inexpensive and easy on bandwidth?

http://www.labf.com - guys in Finland who make XWinPro. Cheap, cheap, cheap (about
$85/seat, cheaper if you buy more than one), and quite functional. Last I checked,
however, the NFS client for NT was not yet working. Download the demo and check it
out.

Oh, and as regards your (7), I'll probably engender hostility on part of somebody or
other, but I've found Samba to be quite a substantial pain in the butt. It's buggy,
and I've seen the latest version of Samba drop connections constantly with NT
machines specifically (Samba mounted NT shares onto Linux). Add to that a syntax
which is growing increasingly bizarre with each generation, and I'd say walk, don't
run, to an NFS solution for NT. Of course, you can always scour the newsgroups
looking for Samba workarounds and bug patches, if you have the time and stamina.

HTH,
Bill




