to save yourself the trouble of upgrading your kernel and a whole bunch of
subsets, i would upgrade to the latest version of Debian (i'm not a debian
user but i play one on usenet).  it comes with kernel 2.2.  or ftp the new
kernel packages and subsets and update them manually.

as for the booting without a keyboard...  you must setup the OS to accept
serial console logins.  Kernel 2.2 did a better job for serial based
consoles but it is possible with a little hacking for the 2.0 kernels.  edit
/etc/inittab and comment out the mingettys and uncomment the gettys.  you
also have to tell MILO that the console is serial.  here is a sample of my
inittab from my LinuxSparc system (should be the same across platforms):
# Run gettys in standard runlevels
#1:12345:respawn:/sbin/mingetty tty1
#2:2345:respawn:/sbin/mingetty tty2
#3:2345:respawn:/sbin/mingetty tty3
#4:2345:respawn:/sbin/mingetty tty4
#5:2345:respawn:/sbin/mingetty tty5
#6:2345:respawn:/sbin/mingetty tty6
1:12345:respawn:/sbin/getty cua0 9600 vt100
2:12345:respawn:/sbin/getty cua1 9600 vt100


AlphaBIOS and milo dont care about what the console is going to.  you can
actually still run AlphaBIOS through the serial port if you really wanted to
(also if you know the control keys to get around).

i would really recommend using the SRM if booting without a keyboard.  but
that's just my preference.

