michal@gortel.phys.ualberta.ca (Michal Jaegermann) writes:

> Apparently some PCI soundcards cut in hardware IRQ assign lines to four
> bits; Windoze will not use more so this is not a problem there.  The end
> result is that a card gets assigned, say, IRQ 22 and is happily using
> IRQ 6 instead.  PCI bus gets crazy and boom...  Count yourself lucky if

huh, are you sure? My understanding of the thing is that the bus by
itself has four pins for irqs and the card can only choose one of
them. Then there's the mainboard's wiring that ensures that there's no 
conflict. The interrupt controler then makes the connection between
interrupt line and interrupt level. So how can a card "use" IRQ 6,
when there are only INTA...INTC lines on the bus connector?

