Greg Lindahl wrote:
> > I've found that the biggest difference in code efficience comes if the
> > compiler thinks that lvalue and rvalue accesses could be the same
> > address or not.
> 
> This is usually known as the "aliasing problem" -- the compiler isn't
> sure if a given pointer store is the same as a different pointer load,
> so it doesn't re-use values in variables.

exactly. The next part follows from this. As the compiler doesn't dare
reordering loads and stores, it will produce bad code.

> > By feeding the produced assembler into a short Perl
> > script, I was able to identify ugly code, optimize it and shave 30% of
> > the execution time of a 700 line function by optimizing 4 lines.
> >
> > I'll post the perl-script for those who are interested.
> > It finds the lines where the target of a ldt/addt/mult/subt function
> > appears in the following line, which means pipeline stalls:
> 
> .. but this is a different problem, an instruction scheduling
> problem. What I am saying, mostly, is that I obviously don't
> understand what you are saying.

Just enter some test code and look at the produced assembler. 
E.g. a vector addition:
If the compiler is unsure about aliasing, it will produce stalling code:
ldt $f1, 8(a1)
ldt $f2, 16(a2)
addt $f1,$f2,$f1    #STALL, wait for $f2
stt $f1             #STALL, wait for $f1

If the compiler knows there is no such problem (e.g. by using
temporaries or local arrays), it can reorder the instructions and
produce much much faster code.
The scheduling problem follows from the aliasing prob.

> In addition, is this savings in gcc or in ccc?

The savings are in ccc. After I entered all hints to remove aliasing
problems, the ccc produces no more "ugly" code, gcc still does. ccc's
code is about three times as fast.

> Are you using a 21264 or a 21164? The 21264 has enough out-of-order
> execution stuff that the above problem doesn't always cause a stall.

The stuff we are talking about are stores and loads. Does the 21264
analyze those to see that they don't access the same location so they
can be reordered? Still, I would expect no more than a factor of 2
improvement due to ooo execution. The code is still bad. E.g. for matric
multiplication (of small matrices), the good code also uses register
