Organization: Electronics Lab, ETH Zurich, Switzerland
MIME-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

Greg Lindahl wrote:
> 

> > The stuff we are talking about are stores and loads. Does the 21264
> > analyze those to see that they don't access the same location so they
> > can be reordered?
> 
> No. I think the Merced is the first processor to do that.

And I thought, Merced won't do anything out of order, the compiler will
have to prepare everything (I see now how good that is done :-)
I suspect that he will only have a shortcut (from pending write directly
to newly issued read)

> > I was interested to improve my times of 20us per Finite Element. I'm now
> > running at 2.8us per FE with 345 MFLOPS, which is more than a third of
> > peak. That's OK for me. There is not much room for improvement (except
> > increasing clock frequency)
> 
> That's actually really good on an EV56. Most people did not get above
> 250 MFLOPS for C or Fortran. I have generally seen a 2x improvement
> with an EV6 at the same clock, but I suspect you will get worse than
> that since your code is already pretty good.

I should get almost linear improvements with clock frequency. And then
there will be other stuff to worry about, when I connect 24 EV67 @ 667
MHz. Pieces of code that I cannot optimize so easily and will therefore
be glad if they get reordered.
Besides I'm almost at my target. Three years ago, I gazed into my
crystal ball and said "those Finite Elements will take 2.5 us each",
which was rather 
limitless optimism. With slower calculations our project would not have
been doable.
I'm almost there, and with the faster processor I expect to probably be
even better (and then lose a lot of time doing communication and
collision detection...)

- Alex



