Wolfgang Seemann (woeba@doomnet.de) wrote:

: > to boot, the machine frequently crashes displaying a message along the
: > lines of:
: > "pyxis machine check vector=0x670 code=0x98 processor detected hard
: > error"

[...]

: > According to the ARM, this machine check indicates a problem with the
: > processor or on-chip caches (as opposed to memory or peripherals).  I
: > guess my question is, is it really possible that I have a faulty CPU
: > (as opposed to a faulty board/memory/scsi controller)? 

According to the DPW Service guide, the 98h machine check can result from
a fair lot of reasons, namely:

[...]
If ICPERR_STAT<tmr> is set, there was a timeout reset error or a cfail_h/no
cack_h error
If DC_PERR_STAT<lock> is set if a parity error was detected in the Dcache.
Bits <5:2> are locked against further updates.
If EI_STAT<bc_tperr> is set, there was a parity error reading Bcache tag
address RAM. If EI_STAT<bc_tc_perr> is set, there was a parity error reading
Bcache tag control RAM. If EI_STAT<ei_par_err> is set, there was a parity
error in an address and command received by the CPU.
If EI_STAT<seo_hrd_err> = 1, a fill from Bcache or main memory, or a system
address/command received by the CPU has a hard error while one of the hard
error bits is already set.
If EI_STAT<unc_ecc_err> = 1 AND EI_STAT<fil_ird> = 0, an uncorrectable
ECC error occured during a Dstream fill.
If SC_STAT<2:0> not = 0 (sc_tperr), there was a parity error reading Scache
tags.
If SC_STAT<10:3> not = 0 AND SC_STAT<15:11> not = 00001b, there was a
parity error resulting from an Scache read, but not during an IREAD command.
If SC_STAT<scnd_err> = 1, an Scache transaction resulted in an error while
SC_TPERR or SC_DPERR was already set. 
[...]

The service guide also give the faulty component as either the main logic
board or the external cache. If I were you, I would start with giving the
system a *really* good dusting and re-seat CPU and all cache chips (if they
are not soldered on, of course ;-))

Cheers,

/Serge.P

