I still think Linux needs a REAL way to discover multiple e-net cards in a
system!  why can't it be like Digital UNIX?!  plop the card in, boot generic
kernel, rebuild kernel, reboot, and poof...  it's there... (the long way...)

you can use either modules or compiled in static kernel.

if you're using modules,  edit /etc/conf.modules or use linuxconf to
configure another card.  this forces the drivers to search for more net
cards.  but the problem i come across is what if you have more than one of
the same kind!!!??  some cards are ok, but some, like DE435, DE450, DE500's
don't wanna see the next card without performing voodoo on the kernel!  I
eventually got it working but i forgot how!

the other way would be to pass milo arguments to the kernel.  like:
ether=0,0,eth0 ether=0,0,eth1 etc...  this also forces the kernel to make a
pass at probing drivers until one works or not.  doesnt always work unless
you know the exact address and irq that cards sits at.

it all depends on how the driver polls to see what cards are in the system.
if the driver doesnt bother to check certain base addresses, it aint gonna
find it.  i have a few 3com cards that had no problem being seen all at once
on the same system!  mileage my vary.

so be prepared to spend alot of time in front of your machine.


