If you have an old ISA network card en you don't want to use modules,
there's a solution to detect more than one card during booting. You
have to change some lines in the file Space.c in
/usr/src/linux/drivers/net.
Firstly somewhere around line 644 (it depends on your kernel version)
the following is written:

#ifndef ETH0_ADDR
# define ETH0_ADDR 0
#endif
#ifndef ETH0_IRQ
# define ETH0_IRQ 0
#endif

To add another NIC, you have to add the following lines after these
lines:
#ifndef ETH1_ADDR
# define ETH1_ADDR 0
#endif
#ifndef ETH1_IRQ
# define ETH1_IRQ 0
#endif

To add more NIC's, change ETH1 into ETH2, ETH3 or ETH4, etc.

Secondly somewhere around line 673 the following is written:
static struct device eth0_dev = {
    "eth0", 0, 0, 0, 0, ETH0_ADDR, ETH0_IRQ, 0, 0, 0, &eth1_dev,
ethif_probe };

To add another NIC, change the lines 670 and 671 into:
static struct device eth1_dev = {
    "eth1", 0, 0, 0, 0, ETH1_ADDR, ETH1_IRQ, 0, 0, 0, &eth2_dev,
ethif_probe };

To add more NIC's change the lines 669 till 658 according the above
instructions.

This solution works on x86 machines, but I think it's a general
solution.

Mark Hesselink







