Matthias Halfmann wrote:
> 
> I have a problem trying to install dabian on a 164sx motherboard with a
> 533 alpha. I had redhat on this machine and I want to change the install
> to debian. So I already had milo setup and working fine. My problem is
> that I can't get the debian rescue disk to boot up and load the ramdisk
> (which is located on the same disk). Everytime I try to boot debian, cd
  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> or disk It just ends up mounting my old install as /. Has anyone else
> run into this? Or an I doing something completely wrong?

I think you're doing something completely wrong.

You need three separate floppys to start off the Debian install.  There
is a rescue disk, a root disk, and a drivers disk.  These can be created
from the CD on a Wintel box (not NT), using whatever version of rawrite
you like (Several versions are on the CD, in \tools.)

The rescue and drivers disk images for your machine (assuming you meant
SX164) are located on the CD in
\dists\slink\main\disks-alpha\1999-03-05\sx164, or something similar. 
The image files are called drv1440.bin and resc1440.bin.  The root disk
image is not dependent on your machine architecture and is at
\dists\slink\main\disks-alpha\1999-03-05\root1440.bin.  If you correcly
use rawrite to create your disks, the enitre disk contents are
overwritten by the image.  This is why I think you are doing something
wrong.  There is no way to put the rescue disk and root disk images on
the same floppy.

This is only the beginning.  Details on how to proceed are located on
the CD at \dists\slink\main\disks-alpha\Readme, which is a plain text
file.

I found it impossible to do an install directly from the floppy, but
that was on an AS255, which has an antique CD-ROM drive that may not
have been fully usable without the correct drivers in place.  Perhaps on
your machine things are different, but if you have any problems with it
I'd recommend the floppy install.  There are 8 other floppys you need to
create with rawrite to do this, and the images for them are also located
on the CD.

If you have any other questions, you might find it helpful to poke
around at the Debian site (http://www.debian.org) or at Alphalinux
(http://www.alphalinux.org).  Both sites provide extensive
documentation, HOWTOs, FAQs and manuals.  You'll get faster results by
checking those places before coming here; most of the basic questions
are already answered.

-- Chris Csernica



