Yes, this is exactly what is happening.  It doesn't need to be a revelation,
since Compaq have stated exactly the above in this very newsgroup.

: Just the fact that WUs are showing up on the webpage
: doesn't mean that S@H has blessed the client as being correct.  It means
: that someone has their hands on the client and is running it.

Wrong again!  When I client returns a WU, it indicates details such as the
platform on which it has been running.  Obviously, that is how the tables of
results per platform are created.  So, if S@H don't KNOW that the Tru64
clients generate valid results they can simply tell their servers not to
accept results coming from that particular platform.

How do we know that the servers can be programmed in this way?  Well, apart
from the fact that it would be a pretty crap server which couldn't be told
to reject certain clients, we know because it has been done in the past.  Do
you remember the Mac client which due to a bug tended to return all WUs
after only a few seconds of processing?  This caused chaos in the results,
and a lot of clearing up.  Rediculously low average CPU times from Mac users
don't appear any longer because those clients have been locked out and Mac
users forced to upgrade to the fixed version.

Yes, if one has access to the source, one can presumably forge the results
to appear to have come from a different platform.  Perhaps this has already
happened.  But producing a blazingly fast Tru64 client which pretended to be
running under (for example) Solaris (or even OSF for that matter) would not
give Compaq the publicity which presumably at least partly drives their
effort to produce these clients. 

: Personally, I'd be hoping that the delay in getting the new alpha clients
: up on the webpage has to do with the work that they're doing in making
: their setup more secure.  That certainly takes precedence over adding a
: client to the webpage.

Well, since the Tru64 clients started submitting large numbers of results,
the text version download page has been updated at least once (presumably to
adds some other clients).  And as I am getting bored with repeating, if they
don't want the hassle of updating the web page they could just bung it on
the FTP site.  That can't possibly have any effect on their security and
requires only the use of a single 'rcp' command.

:>I am not suggesting that S@H allow Compaq to compile new clients and just
