Hmm, several interesting points here.  The most facinating is your phrase
"Do you understand why that title exists".  Do I deduce from this that there
is a S@H volunteer coordinator?  If so who is it please, I can find no
mention of them on the web page.  Perhaps you meant to indicate if I
understood why the title existed in the abstract rather than specific sense. 
Well, yes strangely I do.  I have in my time been involved with a number of
organisations with a mix of full time and volunteer staff (e.g the British
Red Cross).  Your rather interesting thesis seems to be that a small group
of overworked professional and volunteer people cannot reduce their workload
by recruiting more volunteers because this would produce a net increase in
work.  Hmmm, I suspect that quite an interesting book could be written on
this area of resources management (perhaps one already has, if so can
anyone give me a suitable reference?).  Perhaps there is something unusual
about the situation of S@H.  My personal experience has been that most
overworked organisations welcome well motivated and competent volunteers
(and obviously both of the criteria are important) since the work required
to coordinate them is less than their input to solving the problem,
resulting in a net gain.  This scheme certainly worked for the Red Cross and
also for several theatres with which I have been involved. 

